  "notes": "The technically precise pole of Nashville session drumming, placed in PRODUCERS by the research as an instrumental-grammar entry. The single most useful documented parameter is a pair of influences: \"Jeff Porcaro and Lenny Castro … that means an LA-session/fusion sensibility inside country: immaculate time, tightly controlled dynamics, ghost-note-rich hi-hat and snare work, half-open hat textures, and a **shuffle-inflected 16th feel where the song allows it**.\" Every one of those is authored literally — the highest `quantizeStrength` and lowest `velocityVar` of any drummer entry here, a dense ghost set on both the snare and the hat, a real open-hat probability for the half-open texture, and `session.swing` at 0.54 on a 16th grid, which is the only swung setting in this section and comes straight from that sentence. He also plays \"congas, bongos and djembe, so a percussion overdub layer (not just shaker/tambourine) is in-palette on his sessions\" — hence the hand-percussion lanes. On the arena records \"the drums carry the dynamic map — hats/cross-stick verse, full backbeat chorus, tom-driven bridge, final chorus with doubled snare and continuous crash.\" ⚠ **No `session.bpm` is authored** — the entry gives no tempo range, so the band is inherited rather than invented. ⚠ The entry states \"Melody/chords: n/a,\" so **every number in the melody, countermelody, chords and bassline blocks is INFERRED** — this author's reading of the LA-session ensemble the entry documents, not a researched fact. ⚠ Confidence is medium (research: medium-high).",
